ertices of a square. What is the perimeter of the square?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"d9b398b679c9940b738ad47ada4472487bf82926909b86a52c241fb22aee09a3"},"seconds":7.704375267028809,"seed":401819457244954162,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the perimeter of the square, we first need to determine the side length of the square. The side length can be found by calculating the distance between the two given adjacent vertices of the square, which are \\((1, -2)\\) and \\((-4, 10)\\).\n\nThe distance \\(d\\) between two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is given by the distance formula:\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\n\\]\nSubstituting the given points \\((1, -2)\\) and \\((-4, 10)\\) into the formula, we get:\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(-4 - 1)^2 + (10 - (-2))^2} = \\sqrt{(-5)^2 + (12)^2} = \\sqrt{25 + 144} = \\sqrt{169} = 13\n\\]\nSo, the side length of the square is 13. The perimeter \\(P\\) of a square is given by the formula:\n\\[\nP = 4 \\times \\text{side length}\n\\]\nTherefore, the perimeter of the square is:\n\\[\nP = 4 \\times 13 = 52\n\\]\nThus, the perimeter of the square is \\(\\boxed{52}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{52}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:224"}
